{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Of the family of Manasseh son of Joseph.</b> Why is this said? Was it not already said <b>son of Manasseh</b>? Rather, to tell you: Joseph loved the Land, as it says ״וְהַעֲלִתֶם אֶת עַצְמֹתַי״ and so on (Genesis 50), and his daughters loved the Land, as it says ״תְּנָה לָּנוּ אֲחֻזָּה״. And to teach you that they were all righteous — for anyone whose deeds and whose fathers' deeds are unspecified, yet Scripture specifies one of them to trace his lineage for praise, that one is a righteous person son of a righteous person; and if it traces for blame, as in ״בָּא יִשְׁמָעֵאל בֶּן נְתַנְיָה בֶּן אֱלִישָׁמָע״ (II Kings 25), it is known that all those mentioned with him were wicked (Sifrei)." "<b>Mahlah, Noa, and so on.</b> And further on (Numbers 36:11) it says ״וַתִּהְיֶינָה מַחְלָה תִּרְצָה״, which teaches that all of them were equal — this one to that one; therefore it changed their order (there)."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:13:00.748608104Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}